  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 210 AM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nExtremely hot conditions will continue as the upper high holds\nfirm over the Quad State. No reason to make any changes to the \nExtreme Heat Warning. With a more persistent light wind at \nnight, low temperatures will only be in the middle to upper 70s,\nwhich will not provide much relief from the heat and will \nprovide a quick start to the day's warming today through \nSaturday. Look for temperatures and heat indices to be just a \ndegree or two higher today through Saturday.\n\nDecided to leave the NBM temperatures and dewpoints alone. \nAfternoon dewpoints have held in the upper 60s to lower 70s \nover most of the region, with any lower readings very localized \nand therefore hard to account for in the grids. High \ntemperatures may be a degree or two too low in some places, \nespecially across the north and west, but the resultant heat \nindices seem reasonable, generally in the 103-110 range across \nthe Quad State. \n\nThe persistent extreme heat and low relative humidity will lead\nto rapid drying of vegetation across the Quad State. That will \nlikely result in an uptick in wildfire starts into the weekend.\nThankfully, with light winds rapid spread is not expected.\nHowever, the extreme heat will create very dangerous conditions\nfor those responding to the fires. \n\nAs for records, record maximum low temperatures will be in play\nat most of our climate sites today through Saturday. While, no\nrecord high temperatures are currently forecast, it will be real\nclose at Cape Girardeau and Evansville all three days.\n\nAs the upper ridge amplifies over the Plains, weak northwest\nflow aloft will bring a weak cold front through the Quad State\nlate Saturday into Sunday. Isolated showers and thunderstorms\nwill be possible mainly Saturday afternoon and evening over the\nEvansville Tri State and Pennyrile. Given the extreme heat and\ngeneral lack of moisture, it's reasonable to expect any storms\nto be short-lived with some potential for very localized gusty \nwinds. \n\nWeak surface high pressure will attempt to build across the \nregion for Sunday resulting in north or northeast winds. The NBM\nis still holding onto triple digit heat indices Sunday \nafternoon over much of southeast Missouri and the Purchase Area,\nso a Heat Advisory may need to be issued once the convective \npotential Saturday/Saturday night is pinned down better. \n\nHigh temperatures will only reach the low to mid 90s over much \nof the region Sunday and Monday, but over Carter and Ripley \ncounties in Missouri, upper 90s to 100 degrees may continue \n(50-80% chance). The bigger impact will be dewpoints dropping \nwell into the 60s, with lower 60s common on Monday. \n\nThe upper ridge will build right back over the region beginning\nMonday and will be centered right over the Quad State Tuesday\nthrough Thursday. South winds will return by Tuesday, which will\nsend temperatures back into the middle 90s and heat indices near\n100 over portions of the region Tuesday and Wednesday, and\npossibly into next Thursday. The current forecast would not\nsupport any heat headlines, with the exception of Carter and\nRipley counties which may not see an end to the triple digit\nheat indices through the entire 7 day period through Wednesday.",
